<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">2 November in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 2 November, from 1622 to 2000.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mannheim.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1622">1622 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">404 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Tilly's Imperial-Spanish army captures Mannheim</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, the Imperial-Spanish army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-tserclaes-count-of-tilly">Johann Tserclaes, Count of Tilly</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a> from Protestant troops under the Englishman Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a> Vere. Tilly, a Walloon general, commanded the army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-tserclaes-count-of-tilly">Johann Tserclaes, Count of Tilly</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1783">1783 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">243 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Washington issues his farewell orders to the Continental Army</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">From Rocky Hill, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> issued his Farewell Orders to the Armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Weeks later he resigned his commission, setting a precedent of civilian control.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1889">1889 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">137 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">North and South Dakota become US states</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">President Benjamin Harrison signed proclamations admitting North and South Dakota on the same day. He shuffled the papers so no one would know which was signed first.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1899">1899 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">127 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Boers begin the siege of Ladysmith</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> forces besieged the British garrison at Ladysmith in Natal. The siege lasted 118 days, until Sir Redvers Buller's army relieved the garrison on 28 February 1900 after costly battles at Colenso and Spion Kop.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladysmith-kwazulu-natal">Ladysmith</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1917">1917 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">109 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Balfour Declaration backs a Jewish home in Palestine</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Foreign Secretary Arthur Balfour wrote to Lord Rothschild that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> favoured 'a national home for the Jewish people' in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, without prejudice to existing non-Jewish communities. Its consequences still shape the region.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1930">1930 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">96 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Haile Selassie is crowned Emperor of Ethiopia</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> Tafari Makonnen was crowned Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> I in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> before foreign dignitaries. He ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, with an interruption during Italian occupation, until his overthrow in 1974.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1936">1936 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">90 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The BBC launches the world's first regular high-definition TV service</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">From Alexandra Palace in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, the BBC began a regular public television service in what was then called high definition. It was suspended during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and resumed in 1946.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/2000">2000 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">26 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The first crew moves into the International Space Station</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Expedition 1, an American astronaut and two Russian cosmonauts, docked at the International Space Station. Since then the station has been continuously occupied by humans.</p></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 2 November <span class="n">4</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mahmud-of-ghazni.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Mahmud of Ghazni</span><span class="tp">971–1030</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/yolande-duchess-of-lorraine"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yolande-duchess-of-lorraine.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Yolande, Duchess of Lorraine</span><span class="tp">1428–1483</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/marie-antoinette.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Marie Antoinette</span><span class="tp">1755–1793</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/george-bowen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/george-bowen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">George Bowen</span><span class="tp">1821–1899</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 2 November <span class="n">4</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-flanders"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/matilda-of-flanders.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Matilda of Flanders</span><span class="tp">c. 1031–1083</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ngo-dinh-diem"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ngo-dinh-diem.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Ngo Dinh Diem</span><span class="tp">1901–1963</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/theo-van-gogh-film-director"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Theo van Gogh (film director)</span><span class="tp">1957–2004</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/zayed-bin-sultan-al-nahyan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zayed-bin-sultan-al-nahyan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahyan</span><span class="tp">1918–2004</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
